1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,135
Water is H two O.

2
00:00:02,135 --> 00:00:04,807
Natural gas is mostly CH four.

3
00:00:04,807 --> 00:00:06,998
Common salt is N a C l.

4
00:00:07,398 --> 00:00:12,541
Why does oxygen hold two hydrogens,
carbon four, and sodium one chlorine?

5
00:00:12,991 --> 00:00:15,592
Diya is revising at her study table.

6
00:00:15,592 --> 00:00:19,372
Every formula in her notebook
follows one counting rule.

7
00:00:19,722 --> 00:00:24,247
Each atom has a combining power,
set by its outermost electrons.

8
00:00:24,747 --> 00:00:29,711
By the end, you will predict formulas,
and see how oxygen can be plus two,

9
00:00:29,711 --> 00:00:31,155
and also minus two.

10
00:00:31,805 --> 00:00:33,292
So what is valency?

11
00:00:33,642 --> 00:00:38,887
Valency is the combining power of an
atom: how many bonds it usually forms.

12
00:00:39,237 --> 00:00:43,303
It comes from the outermost
electrons, called valence electrons.

13
00:00:43,653 --> 00:00:48,164
Valency is usually the number of
valence electrons, or eight minus that

14
00:00:48,164 --> 00:00:49,064
number.

15
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:53,055
Sodium has one valence
electron, and gives it away.

16
00:00:53,055 --> 00:00:54,076
Valency one.

17
00:00:54,476 --> 00:00:58,132
Chlorine has seven, and
needs one more to make eight.

18
00:00:58,132 --> 00:00:59,483
Valency one again.

19
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:01,565
One sodium joins one chlorine.

20
00:01:02,215 --> 00:01:06,539
On our molecule bench, here is
carbon, with four valence electrons.

21
00:01:06,889 --> 00:01:09,796
Each electron pairs with one hydrogen.

22
00:01:09,796 --> 00:01:12,043
Four hydrogens, four bonds.

23
00:01:12,043 --> 00:01:13,769
Methane, CH four.

24
00:01:13,769 --> 00:01:14,950
Valency four.

25
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:16,653
Nitrogen has five.

26
00:01:16,653 --> 00:01:20,838
It needs three more to reach
eight, so it takes three hydrogens.

27
00:01:20,838 --> 00:01:22,347
Ammonia, NH three.

28
00:01:22,747 --> 00:01:24,049
Oxygen has six.

29
00:01:24,049 --> 00:01:25,924
Eight minus six is two.

30
00:01:25,924 --> 00:01:27,105
Two hydrogens.

31
00:01:27,105 --> 00:01:28,005
Water.

32
00:01:28,178 --> 00:01:29,707
Fluorine has seven.

33
00:01:29,707 --> 00:01:31,700
Eight minus seven is one.

34
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:32,830
One hydrogen.

35
00:01:32,830 --> 00:01:34,237
Hydrogen fluoride.

36
00:01:34,637 --> 00:01:36,514
Four, three, two, one.

37
00:01:36,514 --> 00:01:40,329
Up to group fourteen, valency
equals the valence electrons.

38
00:01:40,329 --> 00:01:43,290
After that, it is
usually eight minus them.

39
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:48,729
Groups fifteen to seventeen can also
use all their valence electrons.

40
00:01:48,729 --> 00:01:53,506
Phosphorus is three in P four O
six, and five in P four O ten.

41
00:01:53,906 --> 00:01:55,258
Pause and think.

42
00:01:55,258 --> 00:01:59,367
Silicon is in group fourteen,
bromine in group seventeen.

43
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:03,312
Is their compound S i B
r four, or S i four B r?

44
00:02:06,512 --> 00:02:07,948
S i B r four!

45
00:02:07,948 --> 00:02:11,622
Silicon has valency four,
bromine valency one.

46
00:02:12,272 --> 00:02:14,652
Now the big idea: oxidation state.

47
00:02:15,002 --> 00:02:15,921
Step one.

48
00:02:15,921 --> 00:02:20,999
Oxidation state is the charge an atom
gets when every shared electron goes

49
00:02:20,999 --> 00:02:23,465
to the more electronegative atom.

50
00:02:24,215 --> 00:02:25,115
Step two.

51
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:29,936
In OF two, fluorine is four point
zero, oxygen three point five.

52
00:02:29,936 --> 00:02:31,760
Fluorine wins each pair.

53
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:33,486
Step three.

54
00:02:33,486 --> 00:02:35,608
Each fluorine gets minus one.

55
00:02:35,608 --> 00:02:39,374
Two fluorines make minus
two, so oxygen is plus two.

56
00:02:40,124 --> 00:02:41,051
Step four.

57
00:02:41,051 --> 00:02:44,407
In N a two O, oxygen
is the stronger puller.

58
00:02:44,407 --> 00:02:49,446
It takes one electron from each sodium:
oxygen minus two, each sodium plus

59
00:02:49,446 --> 00:02:50,346
one.

60
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:51,699
Step five.

61
00:02:51,699 --> 00:02:54,256
Plus two and minus two make zero.

62
00:02:54,256 --> 00:02:57,376
A neutral compound always adds up to zero.

63
00:02:58,126 --> 00:02:59,420
Pause and think.

64
00:02:59,420 --> 00:03:03,343
Is oxygen's valency different
in OF two and N a two O?

65
00:03:06,543 --> 00:03:07,443
No!

66
00:03:07,071 --> 00:03:11,330
Oxygen forms two bonds in
both, so its valency is two.

67
00:03:11,330 --> 00:03:13,855
Only the oxidation state changes.

68
00:03:14,505 --> 00:03:15,717
Remember this.

69
00:03:15,717 --> 00:03:17,688
Valency counts the bonds.

70
00:03:17,688 --> 00:03:21,914
Oxidation state adds a sign,
decided by electronegativity.

71
00:03:22,614 --> 00:03:24,349
Now, one worked example.

72
00:03:24,649 --> 00:03:27,831
What formula does aluminium
form with sulphur?

73
00:03:28,331 --> 00:03:30,739
Aluminium is in group thirteen.

74
00:03:30,739 --> 00:03:31,967
Valency three.

75
00:03:32,717 --> 00:03:34,786
Sulphur is in group sixteen.

76
00:03:34,786 --> 00:03:37,015
Eight minus six, valency two.

77
00:03:37,765 --> 00:03:41,648
Two aluminiums give six bonds,
three sulphurs take six.

78
00:03:41,648 --> 00:03:44,081
The formula is A l two S three.

79
00:03:44,831 --> 00:03:48,441
Check: aluminium plus three,
times two, is plus six.

80
00:03:48,441 --> 00:03:51,553
Sulphur minus two, times
three, is minus six.

81
00:03:51,553 --> 00:03:52,913
The total is zero.

82
00:03:53,563 --> 00:03:57,772
Some elements show more than one
valency, called variable valency.

83
00:03:57,772 --> 00:04:02,446
It is most common in transition elements
and actinoids, which come later.

84
00:04:02,846 --> 00:04:06,145
And bonds are not always
the oxidation state.

85
00:04:06,145 --> 00:04:11,480
In this aluminium ion, aluminium holds
one chloride and five water molecules,

86
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:13,594
with a charge of plus two.

87
00:04:13,994 --> 00:04:17,522
Aluminium makes six bonds,
so its covalency is six.

88
00:04:17,922 --> 00:04:20,385
But its oxidation state is plus three.

89
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:24,965
Water is neutral, chloride is minus
one, and three minus one gives two.

90
00:04:25,615 --> 00:04:27,081
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:27,431 --> 00:04:31,033
Many students learn that
oxygen is always minus two.

92
00:04:31,433 --> 00:04:35,626
But in OF two, fluorine pulls
harder, so oxygen is plus two.

93
00:04:35,626 --> 00:04:37,733
Check the stronger puller first.

94
00:04:38,383 --> 00:04:40,030
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:45,932
JEE Main has asked oxygen in OF two and
N a two O as a two statement question.

96
00:04:45,932 --> 00:04:48,364
Formula prediction is common too.

97
00:04:48,964 --> 00:04:50,195
Exam level now.

98
00:04:50,195 --> 00:04:53,105
X is in group two, Y in group fifteen.

99
00:04:53,105 --> 00:04:56,537
What is the most likely
formula of their compound?

100
00:04:56,537 --> 00:04:57,820
Pause and solve.

101
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:04,655
The answer is C.

102
00:05:04,655 --> 00:05:05,978
X three Y two.

103
00:05:06,478 --> 00:05:09,335
Group two means two valence electrons.

104
00:05:09,335 --> 00:05:10,918
X has valency two.

105
00:05:11,418 --> 00:05:13,167
Group fifteen has five.

106
00:05:13,167 --> 00:05:16,356
Eight minus five gives
Y a valency of three.

107
00:05:16,856 --> 00:05:19,347
Three X give six, two Y take six.

108
00:05:19,347 --> 00:05:22,290
B swaps the numbers, the most common slip.

109
00:05:22,890 --> 00:05:24,347
Here is your recap.

110
00:05:24,347 --> 00:05:25,844
Screenshot this card.

111
00:05:29,044 --> 00:05:32,042
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

112
00:05:32,442 --> 00:05:33,342
One.

113
00:05:33,031 --> 00:05:36,520
What formula does magnesium
form with chlorine?

114
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:38,220
Two.

115
00:05:37,893 --> 00:05:42,912
Why is oxygen plus two in OF
two, but minus two in N a two O?

116
00:05:43,712 --> 00:05:44,612
Three.

117
00:05:44,321 --> 00:05:48,057
What is the difference between
valency and oxidation state?

118
00:05:48,957 --> 00:05:53,928
Next time: why do so many properties
follow the same arrows on the periodic

119
00:05:53,928 --> 00:05:54,828
table?

120
00:05:54,568 --> 00:05:58,774
We find out in periodic trends
across periods and down groups.
